// =====================================================
// N8N NODES: "Has Signals?" (IF) + "Supabase Signal Insert" (HTTP Request)
// Position: first branch after SIGNAL LOGGER, BEFORE the Telegram branch
// [P-06] Rewritten for paper trading: service_role credential,
//        idempotent insert, Continue on Fail, IF guard.
//
// NOTE: These are NOT Code nodes — configure them in the N8N UI.
// Exact values below. Requires 002_signal_ingest.sql to be applied first
// (the unique index on scan_cycle_id,symbol is what makes on_conflict work).
// =====================================================

// ── 0. CREDENTIAL (create once, no keys in node text) ─
//
// N8N → Credentials → New → "Custom Auth"
// Name: Supabase service_role (crypto)
// JSON:
//   {
//     "headers": {
//       "apikey":        "<SERVICE_ROLE_KEY>",
//       "Authorization": "Bearer <SERVICE_ROLE_KEY>"
//     }
//   }
// Key: Supabase Dashboard → Settings → API → service_role (secret).
// NEVER put the service_role key in a node field, in the repo, or in a browser.
// (Custom Auth is used because Supabase needs BOTH headers, and a Header Auth
//  credential can only carry one.)

// ── 1. IF NODE: "Has Signals?" ────────────────────────
//
// Connected to the SIGNAL LOGGER output. Purpose: SIGNAL LOGGER attaches
// _signal_logger_payload / _signal_logger_count ONLY to item[0] and only when
// there are selected signals. A no-signal cycle (or items 1..N) has no count,
// so they must not reach the insert node.
//
// Condition (Number):
//   Value 1: {{ $json._signal_logger_count ?? 0 }}
//   Operation: is greater than
//   Value 2: 0
//
// TRUE branch  → Supabase Signal Insert
// FALSE branch → leave unconnected (nothing to do)

// ── 2. HTTP REQUEST NODE: "Supabase Signal Insert" ────
//
// Name:    Supabase Signal Insert
// Method:  POST
// URL:     https://<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co/rest/v1/telegram_signals?on_conflict=scan_cycle_id,symbol
//          (replace <PROJECT_REF> — Supabase Dashboard → Settings → API)
//
// Authentication:       Generic Credential Type → Custom Auth
// Credential:           Supabase service_role (crypto)
//
// Send Headers (manual, no secrets):
//   Content-Type: application/json
//   Prefer:       resolution=ignore-duplicates,return=minimal
//
// Body Content Type: JSON
// Specify Body:      Using JSON
// JSON Body (expression):
//   {{ JSON.stringify($json._signal_logger_payload) }}
//
// Behaviour: re-running a cycle (same scan_cycle_id + symbol) inserts nothing
// and returns 201/200 without error (ignore-duplicates).
//
// Settings tab:
//   On Error:  Continue   ("Continue on Fail: ON")
//   Timeout:   10000 ms   (Options → Timeout)
// Reason: a Supabase failure must NEVER stop Telegram. The error stays visible
// in the execution log (node shows the error output) — check it in the daily
// routine (spec P-31).

// ── 3. CANVAS WIRING / EXECUTION ORDER ────────────────
//
// SIGNAL LOGGER is a Code node with ONE output, so "output 1 / output 2" in the
// spec means two CONNECTIONS from that single output:
//
//   SIGNAL RANKING & TOP 5 SELECTION
//         │
//         ▼
//   SIGNAL LOGGER ──┬──► Has Signals? ──(true)──► Supabase Signal Insert
//                   │         (placed ABOVE)
//                   └──► Loop Over Items ──► Send to Telegram
//                             (placed BELOW)
//
// Execution order: set Workflow Settings → Execution Order = "v1 (recommended)".
// In v1, N8N runs branches that leave the same output in top-to-bottom order of
// the target nodes on the canvas. So place "Has Signals?" visually ABOVE
// "Loop Over Items". This guarantees the row is saved before Telegram is sent.
// (Even if order were reversed, correctness is not at risk: the insert is
//  idempotent and Telegram does not depend on it — but the paper position is
//  created by the DB trigger at insert time, so insert-first is preferred.)
//
// Do NOT connect Loop Over Items to the insert node — it must read all items
// from SIGNAL LOGGER, not the single HTTP response.

// ── 4. AFTER ACTIVATION — CHECKS (U) ──────────────────
//
// a) Run a cycle. In Supabase → Table Editor → telegram_signals: N new rows,
//    with entry_min, entry_max, tp*_allocation, sl_distance_pct, total_score,
//    cycle_timestamp filled, and score_snapshot->'weights' containing numbers.
// b) Re-execute the same cycle (or re-send the same payload): row count does
//    NOT increase.
// c) Telegram messages arrive, unchanged in format.
// d) Temporarily break the URL → insert node fails (red) but Telegram still sends.
// e) After 005_security.sql (Part 6): the anon key can no longer write to
//    telegram_signals.
//
// ── RLS NOTE ──────────────────────────────────────────
// service_role bypasses RLS, so no insert policy is needed for N8N.
// The old advice "CREATE POLICY ... FOR INSERT TO anon WITH CHECK (true)" is
// REMOVED: anon must not be able to write. 005_security.sql drops any such policy.
